+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Persistent OAuth callback server
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Why a persistent server? The previous design started a fresh
+# ``HTTPServer`` + ``handle_request()`` thread inside every
+# ``_wait_for_callback`` call. Each server handled exactly one request, then
+# the thread exited while the listening socket stayed bound in
+# ``CLOSE_WAIT``/``TIME_WAIT`` state. When a new OAuth flow started before
+# the old socket fully drained, ``bind()`` raised ``OSError: [Errno 98]
+# Address already in use`` and the flow died with
+# ``OAuthNonInteractiveError`` before the user could ever click the
+# authorize link — leaving the chat stuck on ``awaiting_callback`` and the
+# user seeing nginx ``502``/``504`` errors.
+#
+# The fix: one long-lived ``serve_forever()`` thread per callback port.
+# Each new flow installs its own ``current_result`` dict; the handler
+# writes to whichever dict is currently active and the waiter polls it.
+# Servers are never restarted within a process lifetime — they exit only
+# when the process does (daemon thread).
+
+_callback_servers: dict[int, dict] = {}  # port -> entry dict
+_callback_servers_lock = threading.Lock()
+
